ot 0, ∞, -1. This is a known problem: the minimum number of lines needed to cover all points of a triangular grid is something like n (the number of columns). The solution suggests that the minimal number of sunny lines is 0,1,3. So k=4 is impossible.\n\nThus the solution's answer seems correct.\n\nNow we need to check the solution's reasoning for validity. The solution uses several methods: counting arguments, case analysis, properties of sunny lines (they meet each column at most once), and explicit constructions. Are these valid? Yes, they are recognized mathematical methods.\n\nWe need to check if any method used is invalid. The solution uses a claim: \"A sunny line can meet each column in at most one point (otherwise two points of the same column would have the same x-coordinate, forcing the line to be vertical, which is non‑sunny).\" That's valid.\n\nThe solution also uses a claim: \"If k≤2 then column x=n-2 (which contains three points) cannot be covered solely by sunny lines; consequently one of the non‑sunny lines must be vertical and must be the line x=n-2.\" This is valid because each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, so covering three points with at most two sunny lines is impossible, so we need a non-sunny line covering at least two points. The only non-sunny line that can cover more than one point in a column is vertical. So we need a vertical line x=n-2. That's valid.\n\nThen they argue about column n-1 and point (n,1). The claim: \"A line joining (n,1) with either of the two points of column n-1 is either horizontal (slope 0) or has slope -1; both are non‑sunny.\" That's correct: (n,1) to (n-1,1) is horizontal; (n,1) to (n-1,2) has slope (2-1)/(n-1-n) = 1/(-1) = -1. So indeed any line connecting (n,1) to a point in column n-1 is non-sunny. So (n,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line together with a point of column n-1. So if we don't have a vertical line for column n-1, we need a sunny line covering (n,1) and a point from column n-1, 